{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>For I lift up my hand to heaven</b> (Deuteronomy 32:40). By way of the plain sense, the matter is an oath upon His throne. And He said <b>אֶשָּׂא</b> (\"I lift up\"), for every one who swears lifts up his hand and touches the object by which he swears. And by way of truth, because at the time of the exile He cast from the heavens the earth of Israel's glory, He says that He will now lift it up in a time of favor to the uppermost heavens — and this is the great hand that does battle for Israel. And this is the meaning of <b>and I say: I live forever</b>, for I revive my strong hand when I sharpen my sword alone and steady it to grasp the case of Israel and to take vengeance on my foes, for then the Name will be whole and the throne will be whole, and the enlightened will understand.\n\nNow this Song, which is in our midst as a true and faithful witness, tells in explanation all the events that befall us. It first mentioned the kindness that the Holy One, blessed be He, did with us from the time He took us as His portion, and it mentioned the good things He did for us in the wilderness and that He caused us to inherit the lands of great and mighty nations, and the abundance of goodness and wealth and glory that He gave us to possess in it, and that because of the abundance of all good things they rebelled against HaShem to serve idolatry. And it mentioned the anger that was before Him upon them until He sent among them in their land plague and famine and evil beast and sword, and after that scattered them in every direction and to every corner. And it is known that all this came to pass and was so.\n\nAnd the Song said that in the end He will return vengeance upon His adversaries and recompense those who hate Him. And the reason is that they did all the evils against us out of hatred for the Holy One, blessed be He, for they do not hate Israel because they commit idolatry like them, but rather because they do not do as their deeds, and they serve the Holy One, blessed be He, and keep His commandments and do not intermarry with them and do not eat of their sacrifices and despise their idolatry and root it out from their places — as it says (Psalms 44:22), <b>For Your sake we are killed all the day</b>. If so, out of hatred for the Holy One, blessed be He, they do against us all these evils, and they are His adversaries and haters, and it is upon Him to take vengeance from them.\n\nAnd this is a clear matter, that it promises about the future redemption, for in the building of the Second Temple the nations did not rejoice — His people — but rather mocked them (Nehemiah 3:34), <b>What are these feeble Jews doing?</b>, and their great men were servants in the palace of the king of Babylon, and all of them were subjugated to him. And in those days He did not return vengeance to His adversaries nor atone for His land, His people.\n\nNow there is no condition of repentance and service in this Song; rather, it is a deed of testimony that the evils have been done and we are guilty, and that He, blessed be He, will deal with us with chastisements of wrath but will not obliterate our memory, and He will again relent and exact punishment from the enemies with His hard and great and strong sword, and He will atone for our sins for the sake of His Name.\n\nIf so, this Song is an explicit promise regarding the future redemption, of necessity according to all sects. And thus they said in the Sifre (Ha'azinu 43): Great is this Song, for it contains what is now and what is past and what is yet to come, and it contains matters of this world and matters of the world to come.\n\nAnd to this the verse alluded, for it says (Deuteronomy 32:44), <b>And Moses came and spoke all the words of this Song in the ears of the people</b> — it mentioned \"all\" to tell that it encompasses all the future events for them, and if it is small in speech, for he explained to them its many matters. And even if this Song were the writing of one of the seers of the stars who declared from the beginning the end, it would still be worthy to believe in it, because all its words have been fulfilled until now — not one thing has fallen. And how much more so do we believe and look forward with all our heart to the words of Elohim from the mouth of His faithful prophet in all His house, than whom there was none like him before or after — upon him be peace."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The phrase 'שְׁנֵּן בְּרַק חַרְבִּי' (literally 'when I sharpen my sword alone') echoes Deuteronomy 32:41 where both 'שֵׁנַן' and 'בָּרָק' appear; rendered here literally as 'sharpen my sword alone' to preserve the Deuteronomic echo. In 'כְּמַעֲשֵיהֶם' and 'וְלֹא יִתְחַתְּנוּ בָּהֶם,' 'הֵם' refers to the nations; 'intermarry' for הִתְחַתֵּן follows standard rabbinic usage. 'עַל כָּרְחָן שֶׁל מִינִין' means 'against the will of all sects/heretics,' i.e., even dissenters must concede the point."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:11.429517144Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}